\begin_layout Subsection
Configuration layer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A single file,
 
\family typewriter
config.py
\family default
,
 selects how the program runs.
 The main parameter chooses between the local simulator and a real IBM quantum processor.
 Further parameters define the IBM channel,
 the account instance,
 the access token,
 the name of the device and the number of shots per circuit.
 Every other module reads this file,
 so switching from simulation to hardware requires editing only one place,
 and no experiment contains code that is specific to a backend.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Quantum backend layer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The module 
\family typewriter
quantum_backend.py
\family default
 hides the differences between the simulator and the real device behind a common set of functions.
 These functions run the circuit that generates the deck,
 compute the Bloch vectors of the two qubits,
 return the joint outcome probabilities and the correlators ⟨XX⟩,
 ⟨YY⟩ and ⟨ZZ⟩,
 and compute the entanglement witness and the noise index.
 On the simulator the quantities are computed exactly from the state vector.
 On the real device they are estimated from measurements,
 using sampling for the probabilities and expectation values of Pauli operators for the vectors and correlators.
 Quantities that exist only in simulation,
 such as the state vector and the exact concurrence,
 are not available on hardware,
 and the backend returns an empty value in their place.
 The graphical code recognizes this case and adapts the content of the window.
 The module also contains small helper functions,
 among them a random-angle generator based on quantum measurements,
 which assigns the entanglement angle used in experiment 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Visualization layer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The module 
\family typewriter
edu_viz.py
\family default
 collects the educational charts as independent functions,
 each drawing one chart on a given 
\family typewriter
Matplotlib
\family default
 axis.
 They produce the boxed title,
 the joint probability chart,
 the per-qubit probability chart,
 the density matrix grid,
 the correlator chart and the concurrence trend.
 These functions know nothing about the game or about the graphical toolkit,
 so each experiment can freely choose which of them to combine in its result window.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Game and interface layer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each experiment is a separate script,
 derived from the original memory game,
 and all five have the same structure.
 A function converts the measured bits of the deck circuit into cards.
 The main window,
 built with 
\family typewriter
PyQt6
\family default
,
 manages the grid of sixteen buttons,
 the flipping of cards,
 the matching rule and the hiding of non-matching pairs.
 When a pair is matched,
 a single method builds the two-qubit circuit of the experiment,
 requests the results from the backend,
 composes the charts and shows them in a dialog that embeds the 
\family typewriter
Matplotlib
\family default
 figure in a Qt window.
 This method is the only part that changes substantially from one experiment to another.
 The graphical interface is identical in all of them,
 in accordance with the design constraint of modifying only the circuit and its initialization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Flow of a match
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the player turns two cards,
 the game checks the matching rule.
 If the cards match,
 the experiment circuit is built,
 including the conditional gates that depend on the sign of the first card and on the parity of the emoji index.
 The circuit is then executed by the backend,
 on the simulator or on the quantum processor according to the configuration.
 A new circuit is used for this purpose,
 because the measurement that generated the deck has already destroyed the state of the original qubits.
 The results are finally drawn as charts in the result window.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Design rationale
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Four choices follow from this structure;
 the separation of concerns keeps 
\emph on
configuration
\emph default
,
 
\emph on
quantum computation
\emph default
,
 
\emph on
drawing
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
game logic
\emph default
 in distinct modules,
 so that a change in one does not affect the others.
 The abstraction of the backend gives simulator and hardware the same interface,
 which makes the direct comparison of the two modes,
 presented in the results chapter,
 possible with identical code.
 The minimal modification of the original script keeps the interface familiar to the player and limits the differences between experiments to the quantum circuit.
 Finally,
 the graceful handling of unavailable quantities allows the same window code to show exact values in simulation and estimated values on hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Software design choices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The application is written in Python 3 and uses four main libraries.
 Qiskit and its Aer simulator provide the quantum circuits and the local simulation.
 The package 
\family typewriter
qiskit-ibm-runtime
\family default
 gives access to real IBM processors.
 
\family typewriter
PyQt6
\family default
 provides the graphical interface,
 and 
\family typewriter
Matplotlib
\family default
 draws the educational charts,
 which are embedded in Qt windows.
 Python was chosen because all of these libraries are available for it and because it is the language of the Qiskit ecosystem.
 Each listing is identified by file,
 function and line range,
 and all files are in the 
\family typewriter
src
\family default
 folder of the repository.
\end_layout

