\begin_layout Standard
Here the sign of the first card controls the phase gate,
 and the parity of the emoji index controls the bit flip.
 The parity of the emoji index is used because,
 under the matching rule,
 a pair always contains one card of each sign,
 so the signs alone would allow only two of the four Bell states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Adaptation to the limits of hardware
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A real processor does not return the state vector,
 so the backend functions return an empty value for quantities that exist only in simulation.
 The window code tests for it and shows different content:
 exact amplitudes and concurrence in simulation,
 and measured quantities (witness,
 correlators,
 noise index) on hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Numerical robustness
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One function deals with a numerical problem of the library.
 The concurrence function of Qiskit can return a value that is not a number for almost unentangled states,
 because rounding errors make the argument of a square root slightly negative.
 All concurrence calls go through the wrapper safe_concurrence (Listing 5),
 which clips the result to the interval from 0 to 1.
\end_layout

